      "our_prose": "Plant a dormant, own-rooted bare-root or container fig in late winter to early spring, once the hardest cold has passed, or in fall to early winter in mild regions. Because figs are grown on their own roots there is no graft union to keep above grade; you can even set the plant an inch or two deeper than it grew to encourage a well-anchored, multi-stem bush, the form that best recovers from winter dieback. Dig a hole wide enough to spread the roots, backfill with native soil, water in well, and mulch, keeping mulch off the stems. Do not fertilize in the hole.",
